SSH in.
    root@ubuntu:~$ ssh bandit11@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt, where all lowercase (a-z) and uppercase (A-Z) letters have been rotated by 13 positions

Check it
    bandit11@bandit:~$ ls
    data.txt
    bandit11@bandit:~$ cat data.txt 
    Gur cnffjbeq vf TEBbmJCB8DlA0zTewHxVQ0JPLxMvDkeA

To get the password we must swap all lowercase and uppercase letters by 13 positions, this is commonly known as the ceaser cipher
We use the tr command to achieve this.
    bandit11@bandit:~$ cat data.txt | tr "A-Za-z" "N-ZA-Mn-za-m"
    The password is GROozWPO8QyN0mGrjUkID0WCYkZiQxrN
The tr command swaps 2 lists of characters tr (set1) (set2) e.g tr abc zxy would equal a=z b=x c=y when output.
In this case we need to swap the characters by 13 positions which includes for all upper and lowercase characters.

In theory=
tr "A-Z" "N-ZA-M"
This would swap the alphabet
ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ
with the last 13 characters of the alphabet starting first to make a 13 position swap
NOPQRSTUVWXYZABCDEFGHIJKLMN

This command would only swap the uppercase letters and would look something like this
    bandit11@bandit:~$ cat data.txt | tr "A-Z" "N-ZA-M"
    Tur cnffjbeq vf GRObmWPO8QlN0zGewUxID0WCYxZvQkeN

We do the exact same for the lowercase letters 
    bandit11@bandit:~$ cat data.txt | tr "a-z" "n-za-m"
    Ghe password is TEBozJCB8DyA0mTrjHkVQ0JPLkMiDxrA

Then to solve for the password, we combine both solutions.
    bandit11@bandit:~$ cat data.txt | tr "A-Za-z" "N-ZA-Mn-za-m"
    The password is GROozWPO8QyN0mGrjUkID0WCYkZiQxrN
